Phase 1: High-Risk Kitchen & Dining (1–12)
These topics have high "intent" as people are worried about what touches their food.

PFAS (Forever Chemicals): Found in non-stick pans and microwave popcorn bags.

Bisphenol A (BPA): Common in plastic storage containers and can liners.

Phthalates: Used as plasticizers in food packaging and wraps.

Melamine: Often used in "unbreakable" plates; concerns about leaching when heated.

Aluminum Foil: Safe for most use, but trending searches for its link to acidic foods.

Cast Iron (Seasoning): Is the burnt-on oil "safe" or carcinogenic?

Silicone Bakeware: Questions on its stability at high heat (above 425°F).

Copper (Unlined): Toxicity risks when used with acidic ingredients.

Antimony: Found in some PET plastic beverage bottles.

Teflon (PTFE): Specifically "off-gassing" risks when a pan is left on high heat.

Plastic Takeout Containers: The safety of reheating "black plastic" specifically.

Bamboo Fiber Plates: Often held together with formaldehyde-based glues.

Phase 2: Household Surfaces & Fabrics (13–25)
Focus on 2026 "Clean Home" trends and the "Microbiome-friendly" shift. 13. Formaldehyde: Found in pressed wood (MDF) furniture and some textiles. 14. Microplastics in Laundry: How synthetic fabrics shed during every wash. 15. Flame Retardants: Common in couch foam, mattresses, and children's pajamas. 16. Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs): Emitted by new carpets and fresh paint. 17. Asbestos (Legacy): Still found in old floor tiles and ceiling textures. 18. Polyurethane Foam: The "off-gassing" smell of new memory foam mattresses. 19. Vinyl (PVC) Flooring: Concerns about phthalates in home play areas. 20. Azo Dyes: Found in fast-fashion clothing; potential to release carcinogens. 21. Mercury in Lightbulbs: Safety protocols for breaking a CFL bulb. 22. Radon Gas: The "material" naturally occurring in basements. 23. Lead in Ceramic Glazes: Risks in vintage or imported mugs and plates. 24. Fiberglass: The hidden danger when mattress covers are removed. 25. Talc: Ongoing 2026 litigation regarding asbestos contamination in powders.

Phase 3: Personal Care & Chemicals (26–38)
2026 shoppers are increasingly prioritizing "bio-based" and "non-toxic" ingredients. 26. Parabens: Preservatives in shampoos and lotions linked to hormone disruption. 27. Triclosan: Found in antibacterial soaps and even some yoga mats. 28. Fragrance/Parfum: The "mystery blend" of chemicals in air fresheners. 29. Methylene Glycol: A hidden form of formaldehyde in hair straighteners. 30. Sodium Lauryl Sulfate (SLS): The "foaming agent" that causes skin irritation. 31. Aluminum in Deodorant: The long-standing debate on its safety. 32. Phthalates in Cosmetics: How they help scents last longer on skin. 33. P-phenylenediamine (PPD): The toxic component in many hair dyes. 34. Titanium Dioxide: Concerns about its safety in "whitening" toothpaste. 35. Nail Polish (The Toxic Trio): Formaldehyde, Toluene, and Dibutyl Phthalate. 36. Aerosol Propellants: Respiratory risks from spray-on sunscreens and cleaners. 37. Dental Floss PFAS: Some brands use PFAS to make floss "glide" easier. 38. Microbeads: Now largely banned, but still found in older "exfoliating" products.

Phase 4: Cleaning & Maintenance (39–50)
New 2026 regulations are banning specific chemicals in home cleaners. 39. Chlorine Bleach: Fumes and the danger of mixing with ammonia. 40. Quaternary Ammonium (Quats): Found in disinfecting wipes; skin/lung irritants. 41. Ammonia: Common in window cleaners; dangerous for those with asthma. 42. Perchloroethylene: The solvent used in "Dry Cleaning" that lingers on clothes. 43. 2-Butoxyethanol: The "sweet smell" in many multipurpose cleaners. 44. Caustic Soda (Lye): Used in oven and drain cleaners; causes severe burns. 45. Phosphates: Now restricted in dishwasher detergents but still in some specialty cleaners. 46. Methylene Chloride: Found in paint strippers; highly toxic to the heart. 47. Trichloroethylene (TCE): Used in degreasers and spot cleaners; carcinogenic. 48. Antifreeze (Ethylene Glycol): Highly toxic to pets and children due to its sweet taste. 49. Pesticides (Home Use): Residue from bug sprays used inside the kitchen. 50. Air Fresheners: Phthalates and formaldehydes hidden in "Spring Scent" sprays.

